WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.290
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.960
In this lesson, we’ll learn about the different
PyMOL input and output file types, and how

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.719
to export a publication quality image.

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:19.230
In the last part, we’ll learn to work with
scenes, one of the best ways to save work

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.230
and time.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.240
Let’s go!

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:28.910
We’ll start in the RCSB PDB with the structure
of carbonic anhydrase from the previous lesson,

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.870
PDB ID 3UYN.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:38.160
Given the beautiful, complex looking structures
we’ve seen in the first two lessons, you

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.940
might be surprised to learn that the input
file type is simply a text file.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:52.300
The original files were dot PDB, but a new
file type called CIF has replaced that.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.890
The details of how these files differ is important
to a structural biologist, but a little beyond

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.290
my scope.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:06.080
If you’re interested in these nuances though,
check out the video description for a link

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.400
to this PDB-101 page explaining them.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.340
From the RCSB PDB page, let’s view the CIF
file for the structure.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:23.570
We’ve got some general information about
the citation first, some structural information

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:30.210
that PyMOL reads to set up the cartoon renderings,
and near the end, the file gives information

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:36.409
about the 3D coordinates of each atom in every
residue of the structure.

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:40.870
That’s all PyMOL needs to create great images.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:45.170
We’re going to use the file we saved in
Lesson 2.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:51.140
If you didn’t work through that episode,
pause this video, go to the video description,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.360
and copy the command line code at the end.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:00.580
Paste that into the command line, press enter,
and you’ll be all caught up!

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:07.110
Now save using the dropdown file menu, or
by pressing control S on your keyboard.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:13.430
This saves a PyMOL session file, with a dot
PSE at the end of the file name.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:20.140
PSE files open in PyMOL and can be shared
with others helping you create images.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:25.890
Speaking of images, PyMOL exports PNG image
files.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:32.760
To prep this structure for a publication quality
image, we’ll first change the background.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.230
In the dropdown menu: Display, background,
white.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.870
The color white is preferred because manuscripts
have a white background, and the image appears

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.540
to float against it.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:53.340
Black backgrounds also take a lot of printer
ink, so back in the day when we printed everything

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.069
it was very important to avoid black.

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:58.700
This next part is tricky.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:05.520
Reposition the image and try to show each
and every active site side chain clearly.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.550
We don’t want any hidden behind anything
else, and we want to avoid an edge on view,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:16.370
like how we can’t see this tryptophan side
chain here clearly.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.190
The clipping we learned about in Lesson 1
can be helpful.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:27.100
Scroll two fingers up and down on the trackpad
to adjust, or use the scroll wheel on a mouse.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:34.090
Cut off cartoon renderings look pretty bad
too, so this part takes time to get just right.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.010
Ok, I think this is the best I can do with
this structure.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.090
Now it’s time to save the PNG.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:51.430
We can use the file menu, export image as,
PNG, and I get some options to choose from.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:58.519
But the way I normally save is using the external
GUI buttons at the top right.

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:06.040
Click Dray/Ray and you can adjust the PNG
to be as small or as large as you need.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:11.980
If you have the option to ray, this will add
a transparent background and make a very sharp

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.510
image.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:18.630
It gives some additional depth, and makes
it look like there’s a light shining on

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.500
the structure.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:27.040
This creates shadows, like we see right here,
which may not be preferred since there’s,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.910
well, actually no spotlight within a cell.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:37.229
Whether you chose to draw or ray here, you’ll
get the option to save the PNG or copy the

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:42.290
image directly to the clipboard, which I find
very useful.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:47.830
Not to be dramatic, but the day I learned
to save scenes revolutionized my PyMOL modeling

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:48.830
experience.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:55.289
It is so easy to mis-click and mess up your
structure, and scenes give you a quick and

00:04:55.289 --> 00:04:57.020
easy way to go back.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.880
Every time I like the way the structure looks
while I’m modeling, I save a scene.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:08.210
Go to the dropdown SCENE menu, store, select
F1.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:12.780
The scene appears in the bottom left of the
structure viewer window.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:18.600
Right click and “rename” to give your
scene a more memorable name.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.660
The old scene name appears in the upper left-hand
side of the structure viewer window.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.680
Delete that, and let’s call this “active_site”.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:33.910
If you make a small adjustment to the structure
and you want to update the active site scene,

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.590
right click and choose “update”.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:41.250
Let’s change the whole rendering to see
how scenes work.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:46.670
Next to 3UYN in the names panel: Action button,
A, zoom.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.140
This zooms out to show the entire protein.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.090
Now, S button.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.720
Show as, surface.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.200
Rotate the protein and reorient it a little
bit.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.350
Let’s color it a solid color.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.570
Now, save a scene.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.780
Scene dropdown, store, F1.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:12.660
Right-click on F1 to rename it to surface,
or whatever you want.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:19.699
Now, left click on the two scene names to
toggle between them.

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:20.699
Neat!

00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:26.060
You can always return to the exact view from
any scene you’ve saved.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:32.810
Don’t forget to control S now and save your
whole PSE session file with the new scenes

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:33.810
in it.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.009
Thanks for watching this episode of PyMOL
101.

00:06:37.009 --> 00:06:42.180
Feel free to leave me a comment or a question,
and if you’re enjoying this series, subscribe

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:43.819
to my channel.

00:06:43.819 --> 00:06:44.780
Happy modeling!

